  "response": "# Verdict: REVISE\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component implements the visual and structural requirements of the GOV.UK error pattern with high fidelity. However, it contains a **critical design gap** regarding programmatic focus management in the error summary and a **major architectural flaw** regarding \"announcement overload\" caused by the dual-announcement strategy. While it passes automated checks, a keyboard or screen reader user will find the interaction model broken when attempting to navigate from the summary to the fields.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n- **Predicted**: Announcement overload from dual patterns. **Actual**: Confirmed. The combination of `role=\"alert\"` and four separate `aria-live=\"polite\"` regions creates a \"noisy\" experience.\n- **Predicted**: Focus management issues with anchors. **Actual**: Confirmed. The `<a>` tags in the summary scroll the page but do not move programmatic focus to the inputs.\n- **Predicted**: Missing `autocomplete`. **Actual**: Confirmed.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n**1. Error Summary links do not move programmatic focus**\n- **Evidence**: `RegistrationForm.jsx:92` \u2014 `<a href={`#field-${field}`}>{msg}</a>`\n- **User Group**: Keyboard-only, Screen reader users.\n- **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order).\n- **Why this matters**: In a React SPA, a standard anchor link to an ID scrolls the viewport to the element but **does not move the programmatic focus**. A keyboard user clicking \"Email address is required\" will see the page scroll to the email field, but their focus remains on the link in the summary. The next `Tab` press will move them to the next link in the summary, not into the email input. This renders the error summary useless for keyboard navigation.\n- **Fix**: Replace the `<a>` tags with `<button>` elements (styled as links) that explicitly call `.focus()` on the target input via a ref or DOM selector.\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n**1. Announcement Overload (The \"Noise\" Problem)**\n- **Evidence**: `RegistrationForm.jsx:78` (`role=\"alert\"`) and `RegistrationForm.jsx:104` (`aria-live=\"polite\"`).\n- **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n- **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages) / WAI-ARIA \"Broadcast vs Association\" Anti-pattern.\n- **Why this matters**: On submit, the `role=\"alert\"` summary is announced assertively. Simultaneously, four separate `aria-live=\"polite\"` regions are updated with error text. Depending on the screen reader (NVDA/JAWS), the user may hear the summary, followed by a rapid-fire sequence of four individual error messages. This is redundant and overwhelming.\n- **Fix**: Remove the `aria-live=\"polite\"` wrappers from the inline errors. The `aria-describedby` association already ensures the error is read when the user navigates to the field. If real-time \"fix\" announcements are desired, use a single, dedicated status region rather than per-field live regions.\n\n**2. Missing `autocomplete` attributes**\n- **Evidence**: `RegistrationForm.jsx:100` \u2014 `<input ... />`\n- **User Group**: Cognitive accessibility, Motor impaired users.\n- **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.5 (Identify Input Purpose).\n- **Why this matters**: For common fields like email and password, `autocomplete` allows browsers to autofill data, reducing cognitive load and physical effort.\n- **Fix**: Add `autocomplete=\"email\"` to the email field and `autocomplete=\"new-password\"` to the password/confirm password fields.\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (Friction but Workaround Exists)\n\n- **Redundant \"Error\" prefix**: The summary header says \"There are X errors\", and each link repeats the error message. While functional, it is slightly verbose.\n- **Lack of `aria-atomic`**: The `aria-live` regions lack `aria-atomic=\"true\"`, meaning some screen readers might only announce the changed text rather than the full error message if the message were to update partially.\n\n---\n\n## Enhancements (Best Practice)\n\n- **Input Mode**: Add `inputMode=\"email\"` to the email field to trigger the optimized mobile keyboard.\n- **Password Visibility**: Add a \"Show Password\" toggle to assist users with cognitive or motor impairments in verifying their input.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n- **Programmatic Focus Shift**: As noted in the Critical finding, the link $\\rightarrow$ input transition is visual-only, not programmatic.\n- **Confirmation of Success**: There is no `aria-live` announcement or focus shift for a successful submission state (though the `submitted` state is tracked in code).\n- **Field-level \"Fixed\" announcement**: While the developer intended for `aria-live=\"polite\"` to announce when an error is cleared, the current implementation only removes the element. Some screen readers will not announce the *absence* of an element; they only announce *changes* to content within an existing region.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Dual announcement patterns + broken focus links |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | Focus does not move to fields from summary |\n| Low vision | **LOW** | Contrast and focus indicators are excellent |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Missing autocomplete; error messages are clear |\n| Vestibular | **LOW** | No motion/animation |\n| Auditory | **LOW** | No media content |\n| Environmental | **LOW** | High contrast colors used for errors |\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Will experience a \"wall of sound\" upon submission and will be stranded in the error summary when trying to use the links to jump to fields.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Will find the \"links\" in the summary frustrating as they don't actually move the focus, forcing them to Tab through the entire form manually anyway.\n- **Low vision user**: Will benefit from the high-contrast red borders and clear focus outlines.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\nThe component is \"visually accessible\" and passes automated tests, but it fails the **real-world interaction model**. The most egregious issue is the use of standard HTML anchors for error navigation in a React app; this is a classic \"80% implementation\" where the developer knows the pattern (links in summary) but misses the technical requirement (programmatic focus shift). Additionally, the \"Dual Announcement\" design, while well-intentioned, violates the principle of \"concise communication\" by creating an announcement storm.\n\n**To upgrade to ACCEPT:**\n1. Implement JS-based focus management for the error summary links.\n2. Consolidate or remove the per-field `aria-live` regions to prevent announcement overload.\n3. Add `autocomplete` attributes to the inputs.",
